    {
      "id": "kgccxnq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Immunity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "quf7q73",
      "content": "Immunity is the body's ability to resist infection. It can be innate (natural) or acquired."
    },
    {
      "id": "xj8esrp",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type of Immunity</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Innate Immunity</p>",
            "<p>Present at birth; non-specific defense</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Active Immunity</p>",
            "<p>Develops after infection or vaccination</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Passive Immunity</p>",
            "<p>Transfer of antibodies from another source</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "High frequency topic: Distinguish between Sterilization (absolute destruction of all forms of microbial life) and Disinfection.",
        "Frequently tested: Classification of Bacteria, Viruses, Fungi, and Parasites along with their primary modes of transmission.",
        "Key clinical concept: Modes of prevention and causative agents of Hospital Acquired (Nosocomial) Infections."
      ],
      "id": "5vpr04s"
    },
    {
      "id": "e9zejn6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Hospital Acquired Infections (Nosocomial Infections)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eej4jcm",
      "content": "Nosocomial infections are infections acquired in hospitals or healthcare facilities. They are commonly caused by bacteria resistant to antibiotics."
    },
